DIP : states that high-level modules should not depend on low-level modules. Both should depend on abstractions. Abstractions
        should not depend on details. Details should depend on abstractions.
        Means client or Main class should not depend on the implementation of the service class. Instead, both should depend on an
        interface or an abstract class. This principle helps in reducing the coupling between high-level and low-level modules,
        making the system more flexible and easier to maintain.
Example:
        class Main {
            public static void main(String[] args) {
                Service service = new ServiceImpl();
                service.performService();
            }
        }

        interface Service {
            void performService();
        }

        class ServiceImpl implements Service {
            public void performService() {
                System.out.println("Service is being performed.");
            }
        }
In the above example, the Main class depends on the Service interface rather than the ServiceImpl class.
This allows for easy substitution of different implementations of the Service interface without changing the Main class,
adhering to the Dependency Inversion Principle.

Moreover, if we want to add a new implementation of the Service interface, we can do so without modifying the Main class.

In this folder Main/Client is directly talking with db classes if we want to change the db implementation we have to change
the Main/Client class which is not a good practice.
to resolve this issue we create an interface and let the Main/Client class depend on that interface instead of the concrete db classes.
In future is there is a need to change the db implementation we can do that without changing the Main/Client class,
we just need to create a new implementation of the interface and inject it into the Main/Client class.
This way we are following the Dependency Inversion Principle and making our code more flexible and maintainable.

//NOTE: to follow the OCP principal then DIP is the best way to do that because if we want to add a new implementation
of the Service interface, we can do so without modifying the Main class. This allows for easy substitution of different
implementations of the Service interface without changing the Main class, adhering to the Dependency Inversion Principle.

If OCP is the target then DIP is the solution because it allows for easy substitution of different implementations of
the Service interface without changing the Main class, adhering to the Dependency Inversion Principle. This makes
the system more flexible and easier to maintain, as we can add new functionality without modifying existing code.